#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sparse_array

#% text
# `sparse_array(rules)` gives the array with the values of the `position -> value` rules and 0 elsewhere.

#% text
# `sparse_array(rules, dims)` gives an array of the dimensions `dims`.

#% text
# `sparse_array(rules, dims, default)` uses `default` for the other elements.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Positions are lists of indices (or an index for a vector) or patterns such as `[i_, i_]`; the first matching rule wins.

#% item
# The array is built as an ordinary (dense) list, so every list and matrix function applies to it; at most 10^6 elements.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sparse_array([[1, 1] -> 1, [2, 2] -> 2, [3, 3] -> 3])
#% output
#= [[1, 0, 0], [0, 2, 0], [0, 0, 3]]

#% cell
sparse_array([[1, 2] -> x], [2, 3])
#% output
#= [[0, x, 0], [0, 0, 0]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A tridiagonal matrix from patterns:

#% cell
sparse_array([[i_, i_] -> 2, [i_, j_] when abs(i - j) == 1 -> -1], [4, 4])
#% output
#= [[2, -1, 0, 0], [-1, 2, -1, 0], [0, -1, 2, -1], [0, 0, -1, 2]]

#% cell
sparse_array([2 -> 5], 4, 1)
#% output
#= [1, 5, 1, 1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `identity_matrix`, `diagonal_matrix`, `normal`
